1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:05,032
It's two in the morning. Checkout is failing, and every minute costs money.

2
00:00:05,032 --> 00:00:07,797
In the last ten minutes, three changes went live.

3
00:00:07,797 --> 00:00:14,379
Which one broke it? Roll back the wrong one, and you've made it worse, while the real cause keeps burning.

4
00:00:14,381 --> 00:00:23,643
The answer isn't on any one dashboard. It lives in the connections: this deploy touches that service, which feeds this table, which checkout reads.

5
00:00:23,643 --> 00:00:29,410
Dashboards show symptoms. And today's AI agents answer with confidence, not proof.

6
00:00:29,511 --> 00:00:37,050
So we asked one question. What if an agent could never blame anything it couldn't prove as a path through those connections?

7
00:00:37,050 --> 00:00:43,264
That's Faultline. No path, no claim. And the engine that makes that rule possible is FalkorDB.

8
00:00:43,264 --> 00:00:52,443
This cube is the graph of a real system. Open it, and there they are: services, deploys, tables, people, and the proof path between them.

9
00:00:52,540 --> 00:01:00,844
Here's how it works inside FalkorDB. Alerts, deploys, warehouse lineage, docs and people all stream into one graph per tenant.

10
00:01:00,844 --> 00:01:05,694
Every customer gets their own graph, and their own read-only user.

11
00:01:05,694 --> 00:01:14,031
Underneath, FalkorDB stores each kind of relationship as a sparse matrix, and walks the graph with linear algebra.

12
00:01:14,031 --> 00:01:21,698
That's why a whole company graph, 296 thousand nodes and 1.1 million edges, fits in 490 megabytes.

13
00:01:21,698 --> 00:01:28,756
Checkout raises an alert. A breadth-first search runs inside the database and spreads out from it, hop by hop.

14
00:01:28,756 --> 00:01:33,877
That frontier is the blast radius, found in a fraction of a millisecond.

15
00:01:33,877 --> 00:01:41,589
Three deploys landed. For the recommendation deploy, the shortest-path procedure inside FalkorDB returns zero rows.

16
00:01:41,589 --> 00:01:46,379
There is no path to checkout, so it cannot be blamed. Full stop.

17
00:01:46,379 --> 00:01:50,491
But a path is not enough. Max flow turns every edge into a pipe.

18
00:01:50,491 --> 00:01:57,559
The email deploy's pipe can carry only 2 percent of checkout's traffic, far too little to explain this outage.

19
00:01:57,559 --> 00:01:59,409
Rejected, with proof.

20
00:01:59,409 --> 00:02:05,132
Max flow also gives the minimum cut: the smallest change that contains the damage.

21
00:02:05,132 --> 00:02:11,914
Faultline tries it first on a copy of the graph, made with graph copy in 383 milliseconds.

22
00:02:11,914 --> 00:02:20,691
Who should fix it? Centrality over who knows what ranks the people closest to this code, and a spanning forest draws the escalation chain.

23
00:02:20,691 --> 00:02:26,291
And the model can only point. Every read is a read-only query, with a timeout and a limit.

24
00:02:26,291 --> 00:02:34,170
Only after a human approves does one atomic write record the decision, and a separate read-only connection checks it.

25
00:02:34,170 --> 00:02:40,528
Faultline uses all 10 graph algorithms this FalkorDB release ships, and each one changes a decision.

26
00:02:40,528 --> 00:02:47,650
When a look-alike has a fault of its own, only max flow can tell it from the cause: with it, 20 right out of 20.

27
00:02:47,650 --> 00:02:56,199
Without it, 0. Switch FalkorDB off, and Faultline refuses to confirm anything. FalkorDB isn't a feature here. It's the brain.

28
00:02:56,399 --> 00:03:05,731
Now let's watch it live. Checkout errors spike. The incident opens, and a crew of agents gets to work: recall, scope, suspects, investigation.

29
00:03:05,731 --> 00:03:08,201
Every step lands in the graph.

30
00:03:08,201 --> 00:03:14,857
The gate rejects the recommendation deploy. Here's the exact Cypher it ran, and its zero rows.

31
00:03:14,857 --> 00:03:19,876
The email deploy has a path, but a narrow one. Max flow rejects it.

32
00:03:19,876 --> 00:03:29,630
The payment deploy passes every check. Here's the proof path, edge by edge, and every piece of evidence, traced to the agent and the query that produced it.

33
00:03:29,630 --> 00:03:32,108
The smallest containment, tested on a copy.

34
00:03:32,108 --> 00:03:37,663
And the people who should fix it, because they wrote this code and fixed it last time.

35
00:03:37,663 --> 00:03:41,492
A human approves the exact change, bound to its plan hash.

36
00:03:41,492 --> 00:03:48,027
Faultline opens a real pull request on GitHub, merges it, and verifies it through a second channel.

37
00:03:48,027 --> 00:03:54,567
Applied. Then it writes the decision back into FalkorDB, so the next incident starts warm.

38
00:04:07,957 --> 00:04:15,953
Faultline also runs the pager. The moment the gate confirms a cause, it pages whoever the graph says is on call for the owning team.

39
00:04:15,953 --> 00:04:20,575
On their phone: the verdict, the proof path, and who should fix it.

40
00:04:20,575 --> 00:04:26,075
One tap acknowledges it. The escalation stops, and the graph records who answered.

41
00:04:26,075 --> 00:04:33,035
After a verified fix, the on-call can grant a standing approval for exactly this repeat: one use, seven days.

42
00:04:33,035 --> 00:04:37,505
When the same fault comes back, the gate still has to prove the cause.

43
00:04:37,505 --> 00:04:45,704
Then the same fix runs, a second real pull request merges, and it verifies. Handled while you slept. Zero people woken.

44
00:04:55,357 --> 00:05:03,945
The same graph guards the next deploy. This pull request drops payment retries, and it contradicts two decisions the team already made.

45
00:05:03,945 --> 00:05:05,787
Blocked, before it ships.

46
00:05:05,787 --> 00:05:10,732
And it's the team's memory. Who decided the payment timeout, and are they still here?

47
00:05:10,732 --> 00:05:13,544
Every name in the answer comes from a query.

48
00:05:13,544 --> 00:05:17,612
It learns from the people using it, too: the fixes your team prefers,

49
00:05:17,612 --> 00:05:21,621
and why, the guidelines they set, and who actually answers the page.

50
00:05:21,621 --> 00:05:26,000
The next plan puts your team's choice first: moved up from number two.

51
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:31,512
And FalkorDB shows its own work. From inside the engine: the memory each graph takes,

52
00:05:31,512 --> 00:05:37,407
every query's timing from the engine's own telemetry, and the live tail as queries run.

53
00:05:37,407 --> 00:05:50,657
Now, the numbers, honestly. On 140 worlds built to fool it, Faultline made 0 confident wrong blames. It named the right cause in 51 of the 100 worlds with a real cause, and on the rest it said: not sure.

54
00:05:50,657 --> 00:06:04,430
A strong language model, given the same telemetry, found more causes, and blamed an innocent change 37 times. Then we broke a real system: the OpenTelemetry Demo, with its own fault flags and its real telemetry.

55
00:06:04,430 --> 00:06:18,066
Faultline named the right cause in 11 of 20 faults, with 0 wrong blames in 40 windows; a threshold on the same telemetry was wrong 8 times. On call, a wrong rollback costs far more than an honest not sure.

56
00:06:18,066 --> 00:06:25,477
And refusing is cheap: proving there is no path takes FalkorDB 0.053 milliseconds and zero tokens,

57
00:06:25,477 --> 00:06:34,853
while a language model spends 2,522 tokens to guess. And it scales: 250 customer graphs on one engine, 0 leaks between them,

58
00:06:34,853 --> 00:06:40,246
and about 9 milliseconds for the proof path at 10 thousand services.

59
00:06:40,246 --> 00:06:44,094
And it's live right now: 5 out of 5 real runs, verified.

60
00:06:44,094 --> 00:06:51,171
Any agent can use the same read-only tools over M.C.P. Faultline, built on FalkorDB. No path, no claim.

61
00:06:51,171 --> 00:06:55,550
Clone it, run make judge check, and verify it yourself.

